hypotheses.statement:
For every $\varepsilon>0$ there is $C_\varepsilon<\infty$ such that $$\label{eq:main-uniform}
 M_{-2}[f'](r)\le C_\varepsilon(1-r)^{-1-\varepsilon}
 \qquad\left(f\in\mathcal S,\ \frac12\le r<1\right).$$ Moreover, $B_{\mathcal S}(-2)=1$.*

*Let $W\subset\mathbb C$ be any simply connected domain whose boundary in the Riemann sphere contains at least two points. Every conformal bijection $\varphi:W\to\mathbb D$ satisfies $$\int_W|\varphi'(z)|^s\,dA(z)<\infty
 \qquad\left(\frac43<s<4\right).$$ Equivalently, every univalent map $f:\mathbb D\to\mathbb C$ satisfies $$\int_{\mathbb D}|f'(w)|^t\,dA(w)<\infty
 \qquad\left(-2<t<\frac23\right).$$

The space $\mathcal K$ is compact and metrizable. For every integer $j\ge0$, the evaluations $(F,z)\mapsto F^{(j)}(z)$ and $(F,z)\mapsto Q_F^{(j)}(z)$ are jointly continuous on $\mathcal K\times\mathbb H$ and bounded on $\mathcal K\times E$ for every compact $E\subset\mathbb H$. For every $C_0\ge0$, $$|Q_F(x+iy)|\le\frac{(C_0^2+4)^2}{y}
 \quad\bigl(F\in\mathcal K,\ |x|\le C_0,\ 0<y\le1\bigr).
 \label{prelim:strip-bound}$$ In particular the bound is $64/y$ when $|x|\le2$. Also, $$|F(z)|\ge\frac1{16}
 \quad\text{if }F\in\mathcal K\text{ and }|m(z)|\ge\frac12.
 \label{prelim:zero-separation}$$

If $\rho\le2$, then for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is $C_\varepsilon<\infty$ such that $$M_{-2}[f'](r)\le C_\varepsilon(1-r)^{-1-\varepsilon}
 \quad\bigl(f\in\mathcal S,\ 1/2\le r<1\bigr).
 \label{prelim:uniform-disk}$$ The constant depends only on $\varepsilon$.

Suppose $\rho>2$, let $P$ be the weighted law of Lemma <a href="#lem:weighted-law" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:weighted-law">7</a>, and set $k=(\log_2\rho+3)/4$. Then $$\label{eq:root-exception}
  \mathbb E\!\left[
   J_{F,+}(i)\,
   \mathbf 1_{\{(F,-i/k)\notin\mathcal G\}}
  \right]=0.$$

For every real $t\leq-2$ and every $\epsilon>0$, there is $C_{t,\epsilon}<\infty$ such that $$\label{eq:negative-linear-tail}
 M_t[f'](r)\leq C_{t,\epsilon}(1-r)^{-(|t|-1)-\epsilon}
 \qquad\left(f\in\mathcal S,\ \frac12\leq r<1\right).$$ Consequently, $$B_{\mathcal S}(t)=|t|-1\qquad(t\leq-2).$$

For every $0<p<1/2$ there is $C_p<\infty$ such that $$\label{eq:function-means}
  \frac1{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}
       |f(re^{i\theta})|^p\,d\theta\leq C_p
  \qquad(f\in\mathcal S,\ 0<r<1).$$ Moreover, for every $0\leq a<1$ and every $f\in\mathcal S$, $$\label{eq:function-area}
  \int_{\mathbb D}(1+|f(z)|)^a\,dA(z)<\infty.$$

